<ResourceDictionary xmlns:x="http://adamantium/ui/xaml/extensions"
        xmlns:sys="clr-namespace:System;assembly=System.Runtime"
        xmlns="http://adamantium/ui">

    <!-- Editor Pro's NUMBERS - all of them. Colours live in the variant, shapes in Icons, numbers here, and nothing in
         this theme's style sets states a size, a radius, an inset or a font size inline.

         That is the whole shape of the theme: an editor skin is defined by its DENSITY, so the numbers are the design.
         Written as named keys, "make the rows one pixel tighter" is one edit here instead of a hunt through fifty
         templates - which is exactly what Fluent still is (measured: 461 inline numbers across its 49 style sets), and
         what it is to be brought to next. -->

    <!-- ── Density ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         The row is the unit of an editor. 22 against Fluent's 34: a tree, a list and a tab strip all measure
         themselves against it, so one number sets how much of the project fits on screen. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="RowHeight">22</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="RowHeightCompact">18</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="ControlHeight">24</sys:Double>
    <!-- A slider has no chrome to stand up, so it needs less room than a field even here. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="ControlHeightCompact">18</sys:Double>

    <!-- ── Type ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         The SAME SIX ROLES every theme answers - a template asks for "chrome text", and each theme says how big that
         is. The roles are a contract, like the palette's keys: a template that asks for one is included by other
         themes too.

         This theme's answer is a FLAT scale. An editor with six type sizes reads as a web page, so the middle four
         collapse onto the body size and only the two titles step up - the density comes from the rows, not from
         shrinking every fourth label. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizeTitle">15</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizeSubtitle">13</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizeBody">12</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizeChrome">12</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizeCaption">11</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizeMicro">11</sys:Double>

    <!-- ── Shape ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         3, not Fluent's 4-10. A dense row with a large radius wastes its corners on nothing, and at 22 pixels tall the
         curve is most of the edge. Declared as the TYPE the property takes, so a template binds the metric straight
         onto CornerRadius instead of a number being re-typed at every use. -->
    <CornerRadius x:Key="CornerRadiusDefault">3</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="CornerRadiusSmall">2</CornerRadius>
    <!-- A floating surface is allowed a little more, but nothing like Fluent's 8: a panel that reads as a rounded card
         in an editor looks like it is about to be dragged somewhere. -->
    <CornerRadius x:Key="CornerRadiusSurface">4</CornerRadius>
    <!-- SQUARE. A line tab is not a card, so it has no top corners to round - see TabItemStyleSet. -->
    <CornerRadius x:Key="CornerRadiusTabTop">0</CornerRadius>

    <!-- ── Strokes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         One hairline everywhere. Depth in this theme comes from the lines, not from the fills, so there is exactly one
         line weight and it is never doubled. The accent weight is for the ONE edge that marks a selection. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="StrokeThin">1</Thickness>
    <!-- ONE line weight: where Fluent gives a button a heavier edge as part of its affordance, this theme does not -
    	 depth here comes from the lines being consistent, not from some of them being thicker. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="StrokeControl">1</Thickness>
    <sys:Double x:Key="StrokeThinValue">1</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="StrokeAccentValue">2</sys:Double>

    <!-- ── Insets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Horizontal padding is generous where a row is tight: the text needs air on the axis that has room, not on the
         one that does not. -->
    <!-- The same roles Fluent names, answered for a dense skin. Flatter than its: at this row height a pixel of
         optical centring is a twelfth of the row, so the insets are symmetric and the air is horizontal. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingControl">8 2 8 2</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingDropDown">8 2 6 2</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingControlCompact">6 1 6 1</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingField">8 2 8 2</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingFieldFlat">6 0 6 0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingRow">8 0 8 0</Thickness>
    <!-- All four sides stated: Thickness(a b) means (leftTop, rightBottom) here, not (horizontal, vertical).
         The vertical pair is not slack - on a tab folded against a side the label is TURNED, so top and bottom become
         the gap BETWEEN one label and the next down the column. At zero they ran together into one word. It costs a
         docked strip nothing: MinHeight already sets the row's height, and this sits inside it. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingTab">8 4 8 4</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingTight">4 1 4 1</Thickness>

    <!-- Surfaces, answered tightly: an editor's panels are working space, and every pixel of decorative air is a pixel
         of the project the user cannot see. The tab body gets NONE - the content owns its own margins. -->
    <!-- ── Ribbon ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Answered because this theme includes Fluent's ribbon, not because an editor leads with one - it is a toolbar
         here, not the main way anything is done, so every part of it is a step tighter and the large button loses the
         height it spent on standing out. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="RibbonRule">0 0 0 1</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="RibbonTabPadding">10 4</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="RibbonLedgePadding">6 0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="RibbonGroupPadding">4 4 4 2</Thickness>
    <sys:Double x:Key="RibbonChipWidth">22</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="RibbonChipHeight">20</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="RibbonButtonLargeHeight">44</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="RibbonButtonMinWidth">44</sys:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="RibbonButtonPadding">6 4</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="RibbonButtonPaddingSmall">4 1</Thickness>
    <sys:Double x:Key="RibbonMenuItemHeight">34</sys:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="RibbonBackstagePadding">14 0</Thickness>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RibbonSplitLeadingRadius">3 0 0 3</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RibbonSplitTrailingRadius">0 3 3 0</CornerRadius>
    <Thickness x:Key="RibbonSplitDropDownPadding">2 1</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="RibbonSplitDropDownPaddingSmall">1 1</Thickness>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RibbonGalleryItemRadius">2</CornerRadius>
    <Thickness x:Key="RibbonGalleryItemPadding">2</Thickness>

    <!-- ── Busy indicator ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         A quarter smaller across the board. A spinner in an editor appears beside a status line or inside a panel
         header, not in the middle of an empty page, so it has to be the size of a control rather than of a dialog. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="BusySize">28</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="BusyDotSize">7</sys:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="BusyDotGap">2,0</Thickness>
    <sys:Double x:Key="BusyBarWidth">120</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="BusyBarHeight">3</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="BusyBarSegmentWidth">40</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="BusyEqualizerBarWidth">4</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="BusyEqualizerBarHeight">20</sys:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="BusyEqualizerGap">2,0</Thickness>
    <sys:Double x:Key="BusyFlipWidth">13</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="BusyFlipHeight">18</sys:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="BusyFlipGap">3,0</Thickness>
    <sys:Double x:Key="BusyHeartSize">14</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="BusyShimmerWidth">140</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="BusyShimmerHeight">40</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="BusyArcThickness">3</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="BusyRippleThickness">2</sys:Double>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="BusyPieceRadius">2</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="BusyPieceRadiusLarge">2</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="BusyShimmerRadius">3</CornerRadius>

    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingSurface">10</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingPanel">8</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingContent">0</Thickness>
    <sys:Double x:Key="GapSmall">4</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="GapMedium">8</sys:Double>

    <!-- ── Indicators ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         The chevron every list-opener shares (drop-down, overflow, tree expander, submenu), the box a checkbox draws,
         and the ring a radio draws. Named for what they are, not "icon size" - they are not one size. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="DropDownIconSize">8</sys:Double>
    <!-- The square an expander arrow is centred in. Smaller than Fluent's 16 because it has to sit inside a 22-pixel
         row without crowding the label beside it. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="ExpanderBoxSize">12</sys:Double>
    <!-- A command glyph, two pixels down from Fluent's: at this row height a 16-pixel icon crowds the label. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="IconSize">14</sys:Double>
    <!-- The bar down the left edge of a selected row. An editor selects rows constantly - a solid accent fill per
         selection turns a file tree into a stripe pattern - so the mark is an edge and a faint wash. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="SelectionBarWidth">2</sys:Double>

    <!-- ── Designer adorners ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         The frame drawn around a selected or hovered element on the design surface, and the square handle at each of
         a selection's corners. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="AdornerHandleSize">5</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="AdornerSelectionThickness">1</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="AdornerHoverThickness">1</sys:Double>

    <!-- ── Menus ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         The gutter every menu row reserves for an icon or a checkmark, so labels line up whether or not a row has one.
         Narrower than Fluent's 28 because the glyph inside it is smaller too. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="MenuIconGutter">20</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="MenuMinWidth">140</sys:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="MenuCardPadding">2</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="MenuGestureInset">16 0 0 0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="MenuChevronInset">8 0 0 0</Thickness>
    <sys:Double x:Key="CheckBoxSize">14</sys:Double>
    <!-- The "partly" bar across a three-state box, and the air between a box and the label it belongs to. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="CheckMarkWidth">7</sys:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="CheckLabelInset">6 0 0 0</Thickness>
    <!-- The switch: a shorter, flatter pill than Fluent's, sized so it stands no taller than a checkbox beside it in
         the same settings column. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="SwitchWidth">28</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="SwitchHeight">14</sys:Double>
    <!-- The pill's radius as a CornerRadius, not as the height reused: a metric has to be declared in the type the
         property takes, or the value simply does not arrive - and a control whose template did not finish building
         cannot complete a layout pass, which stalls the whole swap rather than looking wrong. -->
    <CornerRadius x:Key="SwitchRadius">7</CornerRadius>
    <sys:Double x:Key="SwitchThumbSize">8</sys:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="SwitchThumbInsetOff">3 0 0 0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="SwitchThumbInsetOn">0 0 3 0</Thickness>
    <sys:Double x:Key="RadioSize">14</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="RadioDotSize">6</sys:Double>

    <!-- ── Scrolling ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         A thin bar: in an editor the scrollbar is a position readout far more often than a grab target. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="ScrollBarThickness">10</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="ScrollThumbMinLength">24</sys:Double>

    <!-- ── Window chrome ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         The title bar is the one place the theme spends vertical space, because it is also the tab strip's ground. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="TitleBarHeight">30</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="TitleBarButtonWidth">40</sys:Double>

    <!-- Control extents. Narrower than Fluent's: an editor puts settings in a side panel, not on a page, and 120 is
         most of that panel. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="ControlMinWidth">96</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="ControlMinLength">96</sys:Double>

    <!-- Tracks and rules, all a notch thinner - the same reason the scrollbar is. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="SliderTrackThickness">3</sys:Double>
    <!-- The handle: a narrow upright bar, not a disc. Its WIDTH is what a hand grabs and its HEIGHT is what makes it
         readable against a three-pixel rail. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="SliderThumbWidth">6</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="SliderThumbLength">14</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="ProgressBarThickness">4</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="TabIndicatorThickness">2</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="RingSize">32</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="RingThickness">3</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="FocusRingOutset">1</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="DragHandleWidth">12</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="DragHandleHeight">16</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="TabStripHeight">26</sys:Double>

    <!-- The drop caret: a hairline with END CAPS. A 1px line alone is ambiguous about where the insertion SPAN starts
         and stops; the caps close it off, which is what lets the line stay this thin. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="DropCaretThickness">1</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="DropCaretCapLength">5</sys:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="DropFrameThickness">1</Thickness>

    <!-- The tooltip is a LABEL, not a card: room to the sides of one line of text, almost none above and below. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="ToolTipPadding">6 2 6 2</Thickness>

    <!-- Caption strip: how far the icon stands off the drag area's edge, and the hairline gutter that separates the
         application's own commands from the window's buttons. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="TitleBarTitleInset">8 0 0 0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="TitleBarIconGap">0 0 6 0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="TitleBarUnderline">0 0 0 1</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="TitleBarGutterMargin">4 6 4 6</Thickness>

    <!-- The window's own frame, its corner grip, and the theme-swap spinner. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="WindowBorderThickness">1</Thickness>
    <sys:Double x:Key="ResizeGripSize">22</sys:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="ResizeGripInset">0 0 3 3</Thickness>
    <sys:Double x:Key="ThemeSpinnerSize">40</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="ThemeSpinnerThickness">3</sys:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="ThemeSpinnerLabelGap">0 14 0 0</Thickness>

    <!-- Docking. The header button is sized for a MOUSE - this skin's whole premise - where Fluent sizes the same
         button for a finger; at six open tool panels that difference is a row of the workspace. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="DockHeaderPadding">6 1 2 1</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="DockHeaderUnderline">0 0 0 1</Thickness>
    <!-- Air between the tab strip and the panel's own frame. Without it the strip's accent indicator ends where the
         frame begins, and at a glance the two are one thick line - two different statements ("this tab" and "this
         panel") sharing a single mark. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="DockStripInset">3 0 3 3</Thickness>
    <sys:Double x:Key="DockHeaderButtonSize">18</sys:Double>
    <Thickness x:Key="DockAreaMargin">1</Thickness>

</ResourceDictionary>
